  "details": "<p>The archive is located in\u00a0<span>House C1 in the Lower City of Til-Barsip.</span><span></span></p>\r\n<p><img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"244\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan84_general_plan_Til-Barsip1_p176.png\" width=\"331\" /></p>\r\n<p><img class=\"image-inline external-image\" height=\"356\" src=\"http://oracc.ub.uni-muenchen.de/atae/images/Plan85_houses_C1-2_Til-Barsip1_p177.png\" width=\"328\" /></p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "In House C1 in the Lower City of Til-Barsip, 22 Assyrian clay tablets, as well as 2 Aramaic texts, were discovered in and around the doorway between Room XI and XII in a secondary position. The earliest text dates to 683 BC, during the reign of Sennacherib (704-681 BC), but most of the documents date later, to the reign of Ashurbanipal (668-ca. 631 BC). The archive belonged to a man called Hanni, with some of the texts also concerning a man named I\u0161tar-duri, and consists of purchase and loan documents as well as some others, such as a juridicial agreement.",
